FIND-001/FIND-002 summary: run.sh's ratio-validation regexes (_modeb_ratio_n, lines ~98/~100) and the
wake-counter file sanitizer (_modeb_next_wake_count, line ~120) accept leading-zero digit strings (e.g.
"08", "09", "1-in-08") as syntactically valid, but bash's arithmetic evaluator ($(( )) / numeric "[ -eq ]")
parses a leading-zero digit string as OCTAL -- and "8"/"9" are not valid octal digits -- raising a runtime
"value too great for base" error. Line 105 circumstantially suppresses stderr around one comparison; line
133 (the actual modulo deciding Mode B) had no equivalent guard.

